#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc6a53a4-8e37-5e81-924b-1e385cd14ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288741} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf8c5b2-b1d6-50a3-97ac-f53d4d6eb0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{1}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288742} {:answer/id #uuid "721915e8-1e4e-51ef-915f-efa3f36d7eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{5},-\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288743} {:answer/id #uuid "df7b9cc4-6d1c-57cf-95cf-8f455ed20d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288744} {:answer/id #uuid "0237db8d-e99e-59b3-83af-bc307b74484c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0237db8d-e99e-59b3-83af-bc307b74484c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288745} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae89664c-33dc-5fe9-b389-27232ac8691f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56d55b4f-72ce-5ee5-9de8-d9307974b602" :question/math-academy-id "q-114346" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008611/assets/df3838ae44d1477a4a0de303972cd26a2ceaad9b213f1d8e4be3a53bd453aeb2.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3x & =2 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=3x+2$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $3,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=3x+2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{4}{3}-2 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 10x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0)$ is  $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbd880a2-7d10-54b3-a20e-36c8ce0c6562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288748} {:answer/id #uuid "1c925840-af98-5293-a158-18de64b924cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288749} {:answer/id #uuid "77096218-1510-53aa-ba99-2cece25e273a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{9}{5},\\frac{2}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288750} {:answer/id #uuid "ee95ac48-a793-5be2-88b8-aa349fc88509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288751} {:answer/id #uuid "388cb135-019f-5948-858a-1b6fa823e314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "388cb135-019f-5948-858a-1b6fa823e314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288752} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee119671-d7ba-5016-9310-e15749d4d416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d186a0b8-cfb0-554c-a5c7-b433b90e0491" :question/math-academy-id "q-114349" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008611/assets/ba46b4f3f458a6be13ea01ca1351658c102732db94e22725890afaaade6fc904.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $5y-10x=5$ that is closest to the point $(-2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-10x & =5 \\\\ 5y & =10x+5 \\\\ y & =2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=2x+1$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $2,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x+1 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{1}{2}x & =2-1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{9}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{9}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $5y-10x=5$ that is closest to the point $(-2,3)$ is  $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dcf41b7-958b-5f40-a621-bc9736375cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288755} {:answer/id #uuid "486f8e76-0a35-5911-a176-8ad71b901e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288756} {:answer/id #uuid "72c9aa6f-038d-5947-a747-fee648d6d2b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288757} {:answer/id #uuid "ba47b6f4-1116-5380-8e28-847dd10c0cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288758} {:answer/id #uuid "a00af17c-fb49-5798-9579-c60050f6a391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "486f8e76-0a35-5911-a176-8ad71b901e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288756} :answer-field/id #uuid "23a0aa31-08e7-5a59-80ba-f19b542d795b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b774691-7eb0-591e-a16e-3aca9ee0bcfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-74420" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008611/assets/6ec04fe0ad996f87dbc07b0883afb3fa7aef1429412a550afe9102d1c22db71b.png)\n\n  \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ and passes through the point $(5,-8).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-3.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+8 & =-3(x-5) \\\\ y+8 & =-3x+15 \\\\ y & =-3x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3 \\\\ y=-3x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-3 & =-3x+7 \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =7+3 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,-2).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(5,-8)$ and $(3,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-5)^{2}+(-2-(-8))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ is $2\\sqrt{10}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a33dfab3-81bf-5fee-94e3-3c7633339521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{15}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288762} {:answer/id #uuid "32d6fcac-72f7-5ecd-a223-3b06c843a4a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{29}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288763} {:answer/id #uuid "a56dd61d-5e4e-50ba-bfee-74ae1ebb7a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-\\frac{13}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288764} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b178ed-3889-5cd7-b417-46b855678559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288765} {:answer/id #uuid "2a09cf91-4618-5c5e-957b-da3f37105e58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8b178ed-3889-5cd7-b417-46b855678559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288765} :answer-field/id #uuid "5161e89e-3259-5133-baec-108e1f3b561a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b8e0f60-8eba-5366-a303-55d3880df894" :question/math-academy-id "q-74421" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008611/assets/67d09cf1ebb51a37c122ea1e0bb53b0bcabedca0251ae4105ebb93dd55d111dd.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ and passes through the point $(-4,-1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x+4) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-8 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =8-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{13}{3}\\cdot \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-5.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=-5.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,-5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1)$ is $(2,-5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33f2da1d-9fad-5d80-a6c6-f54397460284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288769} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6aac72-9505-53be-98fa-6e7d29c83b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288770} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c5aaa8-564a-5055-abda-0505abccc889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288771} {:answer/id #uuid "5eee4826-c0d8-57b2-b805-ffee4f0f49ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288772} {:answer/id #uuid "a74a12c4-ad75-5cc5-a635-64422a008747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33f2da1d-9fad-5d80-a6c6-f54397460284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288769} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dddba98-7e9e-5b36-bb9f-eda5f9aa09c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6c55d79c-0527-5806-9fec-32477e380606" :question/math-academy-id "q-74435" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x & =-2 \\\\ y & =-2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008611/assets/789659bd700bc5f8407d409bc28bf5ce5309369af2aa5ad5973434d47cff74fa.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-2x-2$ and passes through the point $(-3,-6).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-2,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x-2 \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -2x-\\frac{1}{2}x & =2-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-4.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=-4.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(1,-4).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-3,-6)$ and $(1,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-3))}^{2}+(-4-(-6))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2$ is $2\\sqrt{5}.$"}]}
